# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "A tag name admits what TiddlyWiki's tag name admits"

# html.js reads reTagName = /([a-zA-Z0-9\-\$\.]+)/y and then requires whitespace, a slash or a
# close. A name the parser stops short in leaves a character satisfying none of those, and the
# whole tag refuses — TiddlyWiki yields plain text for it.
#
# So an underscore or a colon ends a name and refuses the tag, while a hyphen, a period and a
# dollar stand inside one. VS Code's own HTML grammar excludes the colon the same way; TiddlyWiki
# excludes the underscore as well.
#
# Namespaced markup has its own home. Content declaring itself as image/svg+xml — a typed block,
# or a tiddler of that type — reaches the XML grammar, which names a prefix, its separator and its
# local name separately. Painting a namespaced tag here would claim a construct the wikitext
# parser refuses, in the one place better colouring already stands.

! A hyphen and a period stand inside a tag name

<my-tag>x</my-tag>
#^^^^^^ entity.name.tag.html.tiddlywiki5

<my.tag>x</my.tag>
#^^^^^^ entity.name.tag.html.tiddlywiki5

! An underscore ends the name, so the tag reads as text

<my_tag>x</my_tag>
#^^^^^^ - entity.name.tag.html.tiddlywiki5

! So does a colon, and namespaced markup answers to the XML grammar instead

<my:tag>x</my:tag>
#^^^^^^ - entity.name.tag.html.tiddlywiki5

! So does a name the parser stops short in

<div"unspaced">x</div>
#^^^ - entity.name.tag.html.tiddlywiki5
